Any help would be appreciated!</think>Getting the exact start and end positions of a selection within a `contenteditable` div is notoriously tricky. The main reason is that the `Selection` API deals with **DOM nodes and offsets**, not plain text string indices. 

If a user selects text across multiple HTML elements (like `<b>`, `<i>`, or nested `<div>`s), the browser will return the offset relative to the immediate parent node of the selection, not the entire `contenteditable` div.

To solve this, you need to calculate the "text offset" from the beginning of the `contenteditable` div down to the selection's start and end nodes. Here is a robust, modern solution that works across all major browsers.

### The Solution

```javascript
/**
 * Gets the start and end text offsets of the current selection 
 * within a specific contenteditable element.
 * 
 * @param {HTMLElement} editor - The contenteditable div
 * @returns {{start: number, end: number} | null}
 */
function getSelectionOffsets(editor) {
  const selection = window.getSelection();

  // If there's no selection or it's outside our editor, return null
  if (!selection.rangeCount || selection.isCollapsed) return null;

  const range = selection.getRangeAt(0);

  // Make sure the selection is actually inside our editor
  if (!editor.contains(range.commonAncestorContainer)) return null;

  // Clone the range so we don't mess with the user's visible selection
  const preSelectionRange = range.cloneRange();

  // Collapse the cloned range to the very beginning of the editor
  preSelectionRange.selectNodeContents(editor);

  // Move the end of the cloned range to where the user's selection starts
  preSelectionRange.setEnd(range.startContainer, range.startOffset);

