To achieve the desired layout in a Java Swing application where you have a scrollable text area taking up most of the space and a button always visible at the bottom, you can use a `BorderLayout` combined with a `JScrollPane` for the text area.

Here is a step-by-step guide and code example:

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;

public class SimpleSwingApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create the main frame (window)
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("Simple Swing App");
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        frame.setSize(400, 300);

        // Use BorderLayout for the frame
        frame.setLayout(new BorderLayout());

        // Create a text area and wrap it in a scroll pane
        JTextArea textArea = new JTextArea();
        JScrollPane scrollPane = new JScrollPane(textArea);

        // Create a button
        JButton button = new JButton("Click Me!");

        // Add the scroll pane to the center (takes up remaining space)
        frame.add(scrollPane, BorderLayout.CENTER);

        // Add the button to the south (bottom of the window)
        frame.add(button, BorderLayout.SOUTH);

        // Make the window visible
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
```

